! array literature_facts = The word "bookworm" originally referred to actual insects that ate through books|J.K. Rowling wrote the first Harry Potter book on napkins in a café|Agatha Christie is the best-selling novelist of all time with over 2 billion copies sold|Dr. Seuss wrote Green Eggs and Ham using only 50 different words|The longest sentence in literature is in Les Misérables with 823 words|Shakespeare invented over 1,700 words still used today|The Great Gatsby was initially a commercial failure|Frankenstein was written by Mary Shelley when she was only 18|Ernest Hemingway wrote standing up at a chest-high bookshelf|The first novel ever written was The Tale of Genji by Murasaki Shikibu in 1010|Amazon.com was originally going to be called "Cadabra"|The most translated author in the world is Agatha Christie

+ famous first lines
- Great opening lines include: "It was the best of times, it was the worst of times" (A Tale of Two Cities), "Call me Ishmael" (Moby Dick), "In a hole in the ground there lived a hobbit" (The Hobbit), and "It is a truth universally acknowledged..." (Pride and Prejudice).

+ banned books
- Books have been banned for various reasons throughout history. Commonly challenged works include The Catcher in the Rye, To Kill a Mockingbird, 1984, Brave New World, and The Adventures of Huckleberry Finn. Censorship often reflects social tensions and fears.

+ book awards
- Major literary awards include the Pulitzer Prize, Nobel Prize in Literature, Man Booker Prize, National Book Award, and Hugo Award (science fiction). These awards recognize outstanding achievement and help identify significant literary works.

+ literature movements timeline
- Major literary movements: Medieval (500-1500), Renaissance (1500-1660), Neoclassicism (1660-1798), Romanticism (1798-1870), Realism (1870-1914), Modernism (1914-1945), Postmodernism (1945-present), Contemporary (1960s-present).